{"step_index":44,"source":"MODEL","type":"GENERIC","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-16T22:44:31Z","content":"Created At: 2026-09-17T04:14:31+05:30\nCompleted At: 2026-09-17T04:14:31+05:30\nFile Path: `file:///C:/Users/renu5/.gemini/antigravity/brain/39f7a808-f326-4999-9a66-c185c6afbf59/.system_generated/steps/42/content.md`\nTotal Lines: 139\nTotal Bytes: 5394\nShowing lines 1 to 139\nThe following code has been modified to include a line number before every line, in the format: <line_number>: <original_line>. Please note that any changes targeting the original code should remove the line number, colon, and leading space.\n1: Title: Live Content\n2: \n3: Description: Fetched live\n4: \n5: Source: https://raw.githubusercontent.com/VjiaoBlack/layercake/main/README.md\n6: \n7: ---\n8: \n9: # layercake\n10: \n11: Cut a photo into depth-ordered transparent PNG layers with [Meta's\n12: Segment Anything 2](https://github.com/facebookresearch/sam2). Every output\n13: PNG is the source image's exact dimensions, so layers stack cleanly under\n14: CSS `object-fit: cover; object-position: center`.\n15: \n16: PSD is for Photoshop. **layercake is for CSS.**\n17: \n18: Built for layered hero composites on the web — the kind where text weaves\n19: between a foreground object and the subject, or where parallax layers\n20: separate fore/mid/background. Click a few points per layer, optionally draw\n21: a bounding box, pick edge quality, save. Source dimensions guaranteed.\n22: \n23: ## Why this and not something else\n24: \n25: | Tool | Click-prompted | N layers | Source-dim PNG stack | Local & free |\n26: |---|:-:|:-:|:-:|:-:|\n27: | Photoshop \"Select Subject\" | ✕ | ✕ | N/A | Paid |\n28: | remove.bg / Photoroom / Clipdrop | ✕ | Fg/bg only | ✕ | Cloud |\n29: | iOS \"Lift Subject\" | ✕ | Subject only | ✕ | Local |\n30: | [jhj0517/sam2-playground](https://github.com/jhj0517/sam2-playground) | ✓ | PSD | ✕ | Local |\n31: | 10b.ai RGBA Layers | ✕ | ✓ | ✓ | Cloud, paid |\n32: | **layercake** | **✓** | **✓** | **✓** | **✓** |\n33: \n34: ## Install\n35: \n36: Requires Python 3.10+. Recommended via [uv](https://docs.astral.sh/uv/):\n37: \n38: ```bash\n39: git clone https://github.com/<you>/layercake.git\n40: cd layercake\n41: uv venv --python 3.13 .venv\n42: source .venv/bin/activate\n43: uv pip install -r requirements.txt\n44: ```\n45: \n46: First run downloads SAM 2 weights (~900 MB for `sam2-hiera-large`). Cached\n47: under `~/.cache/huggingface/`. Device auto-detects MPS (Apple silicon),\n48: CUDA, or CPU.\n49: \n50: ## SAM 3 text prompts (optional)\n51: \n52: The Gradio UI includes an opt-in **\"Segment by concept\"** section powered by\n53: [SAM 3](https://github.com/facebookresearch/sam3) (Nov 2025). Type a short\n54: noun phrase (`rope`, `face`, `hand`, `leaves`) and SAM 3 segments **every\n55: instance** of the concept, creating one layer per instance.\n56: \n57: This is genuinely additive to SAM 2 — SAM 2 needs you to know where to click;\n58: SAM 3 takes the concept name and finds it for you. Especially useful for\n59: scenes where the same thing repeats (multiple rope strands, a crowd, a shelf\n60: of objects).\n61: \n62: **One-time setup (weights are gated):**\n63: \n64: 1. Visit [huggingface.co/facebook/sam3](https://huggingface.co/facebook/sam3)\n65:    and click *Agree and access repository*.\n66: 2. Create a read-scope token at\n67:    [huggingface.co/settings/tokens](https://huggingface.co/settings/tokens).\n68: 3. `export HF_TOKEN=hf_...` in your shell, or run `huggingface-cli login`.\n69: \n70: Then relaunch layercake. The first concept segmentation downloads the model\n71: (~3 GB) and caches it under `~/.cache/huggingface/`.\n72: \n73: **Reading the scores:** SAM 3 multiplies each instance's confidence by an\n74: image-level \"is this concept present at all\" confidence, so final scores run\n75: lower than you'd expect — a clearly-visible concept can land at 0.1–0.3 when\n76: the presence head is conservative (thin structures like rope especially).\n77: If a concept you can plainly see returns nothing, lower the score threshold\n78: before rewording the phrase; the default of 0.4 suits prominent subjects,\n79: not fine ones.\n80: \n81: ## Interactive UI (recommended)\n82: \n83: ```bash\n84: python layers_app.py\n85: ```\n86: \n87: Opens at `http://127.0.0.1:7860`. Workflow:\n88: \n89: 1. Upload a source image.\n90: 2. Name a layer (order = depth; **first layer = nearest**) and hit **Add layer**.\n91: 3. With the layer active, click the image. Modes:\n92:    - **include** — positive click: \"this pixel belongs to the layer.\"\n93:    - **exclude** — negative click: \"this pixel does NOT.\"\n94:    - **move** — 1st click picks up the nearest point, 2nd drops it.\n95:    - **box** — 2 clicks mark opposite corners of an axis-aligned bounding box.\n96:    - **erase** — click near a point (or inside the box) to remove it.\n97: 4. Repeat for each layer. Edit points directly in the dataframe (move by\n98:    changing x/y, delete by removing rows).\n99: 5. Hit **Save layers**.\n100: \n101: Output directory gets:\n102: \n103: - `<name>.png` per layer, all source-dim RGBA\n104: - `bg.png` — the inverse of the union of all your layers\n105: - `points.json` — the full spec, for replay via the CLI\n106: - `snippet.html` — ready-to-paste HTML + CSS that stacks the layers\n107: \n108: ## Headless CLI\n109: \n110: Same engine, no UI. Useful in build scripts.\n111: \n112: ```bash\n113: python layers.py input.jpg \\\n114:   --layers '[\n115:     {\"name\": \"foreground\",  \"points\": [[1200, 300], [850, 250]]},\n116:     {\"name\": \"subject\",     \"points\": [[420, 400]], \"box\": [300, 200, 600, 900]},\n117:     {\"name\": \"midground\",   \"points\": [[1100, 800]], \"labels\": [1]}\n118:   ]' \\\n119:   --out out/ \\\n120:   --edges matting \\\n121:   --preview\n122: ```\n123: \n124: Each layer entry supports:\n125: \n126: - `name` (required)\n127: - `points` — list of `[x, y]` in source-image pixel space\n128: - `labels` — per-point include/exclude: `1` or `0` (default all 1)\n129: - `box` — optional axis-aligned `[x1, y1, x2, y2]`\n130: - `concept` — SAM 3 text prompt (`\"rope\"`, `\"face\"`); requires SAM 3 access\n131:   (see above). A concept-only layer expands to one layer per found instance\n132:   (capped at the 10 best after dropping near-duplicates — SAM 3 often\n133:   returns several overlapping instances of the same object, so an instance\n134:   mostly covered by better-scoring ones is skipped),\n135:   named `{name}-1`, `{name}-2`, … best-score-first,\n136:   and the SAM 3 masks are used as-is. A concept **combined with** points/box\n137:   takes the best instance as a warm-start prior an\n138: \n139: \nThe above content shows the entire, complete file contents of the requested file.\n"}
